WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:09.130
Among the pantheon of corporations that have
emerged out of Silicon Valley, Uber occupies

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.730
a special place.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:13.629
The company is worth a hundred billion dollars.

00:00:13.629 --> 00:00:17.340
Hundreds of billions of dollars worth of trips
and delivery orders have been booked using

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.340
its app.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.750
It gets tens of billions of dollars in revenue
each year.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:22.750
Uber is EVERYWHERE.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.900
And the NARRATIVE of Uber is also everywhere.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.410
It says that Uber is “innovative,” that
it can “disrupt” the taxi industry and

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:30.410
make money doing it.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:34.960
And it says that it can offer “flexibility”
and “opportunity” to its army of drivers.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.020
Everybody wins.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.780
There’s one problem: NONE OF THAT is true.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.251
Let’s start with one basic fact.

00:00:41.251 --> 00:00:46.829
For a company so prominent, there’s something
very strange: Uber has NEVER – not once

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:49.219
– turned a single profit.

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:50.219
You might be confused.

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:55.510
How can it be that such a large company, with
such a popular service, has simply NEVER made

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:56.510
money?

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:59.040
When you find out the answer, you discover
something important.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.350
You discover that Uber is fundamentally a
SCAM – a company that exists to scam its

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.270
investors, its users, and, most importantly,
to scam its workers.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:13.250
Despite everything you might hear, Uber is
NOT a technology company.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:18.020
While the company may brag about its sophisticated
algorithm, in reality Uber is JUST ANOTHER

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:19.020
TAXI COMPANY.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.280
There is nothing fundamentally more efficient
about Uber than there was with your local

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.310
taxi service.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:28.770
The business is the same: there’s no real
way to “innovate” someone driving someone

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:34.341
or something from Point A to Point B. Using
an app to match riders with drivers is so

00:01:34.341 --> 00:01:37.619
simple that local taxi companies DO THE SAME
THING.

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:41.729
In fact, Uber is LESS efficient than taxi
companies.

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:46.560
Most normal taxi companies don’t spend huge
amounts on marketing, or lobbying, or corporate

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.810
headquarters; they don’t pay their CEO 45
million dollars a year.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:55.689
Uber has higher costs than traditional taxi
operators in EVERY category except fuel.

00:01:55.689 --> 00:01:58.909
So why is Uber everywhere, while taxi companies
go out of business?

00:01:58.909 --> 00:02:01.619
It all goes back to Uber’s master plan.

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:06.560
First: with massive amounts of money from
venture capitalists, Uber subsidized significantly

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.629
cheaper rides than what taxi companies could
offer, to attract customers away from them.

00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:16.470
Then, after years of undercutting competitors
and driving them to bankruptcy – resulting

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:21.359
in a wave of suicides by cab drivers – Uber
established near-monopolies in local markets.

00:02:21.359 --> 00:02:26.470
Now, Uber is often the default way to get
around if you don’t have a car.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:30.999
And once Uber has eradicated its competition,
it can MAKE ITSELF profitable, at the expense

00:02:30.999 --> 00:02:36.249
of users and drivers – raising its fares
to what taxis offered or higher, while pushing

00:02:36.249 --> 00:02:38.010
down drivers’ wages.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:41.689
The plan isn’t efficiency: it’s monopoly.

00:02:41.689 --> 00:02:43.329
And that’s not just me theorizing.

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:48.970
A 73-page article in the Transportation Law
Journal took a DEEP LOOK at Uber’s business,

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.370
and concluded one thing:

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:56.599
“Uber has NO ABILITY – now or in the foreseeable
future – to earn sustainable profits in

00:02:56.599 --> 00:02:58.120
a competitive marketplace.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.919
Uber’s investors cannot earn returns on
the money they have invested without achieving

00:03:01.919 --> 00:03:06.819
levels of market dominance that would allow
them to exploit anti-competitive market power.”

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:08.749
So THAT is Uber’s grand plan.

00:03:08.749 --> 00:03:13.540
It is not increasing the productivity or efficiency
of the industry that it’s in.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.529
It’s subsidizing its rides with venture
capital cash until it can build a monopoly

00:03:17.529 --> 00:03:19.120
and do whatever it wants.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.420
So users who buy Uber’s narrative may be
getting scammed.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:26.260
But NO ONE is getting it worse than Uber’s
workers.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.279
Uber relies on classifying drivers as “independent
contractors.”

00:03:30.279 --> 00:03:35.859
In the United States, that means an individual
provides services to a company, but is independent

00:03:35.859 --> 00:03:37.549
of the company and its control.

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:42.530
Uber says that because drivers can choose
what work to accept and for how long, they’re

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:43.530
fully independent.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.609
Now, most of Uber’s workers drive part-time.

00:03:46.609 --> 00:03:48.999
But they actually do relatively little driving.

00:03:48.999 --> 00:03:52.040
And 90 percent of them quit each year.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.909
The MAJORITY of Uber’s labor is done by
a smaller group of workers, who drive full-time

00:03:55.909 --> 00:03:56.909
for the company.

00:03:56.909 --> 00:03:58.209
So who are these drivers?

00:03:58.209 --> 00:04:02.939
In cities like New York, the vast majority
are immigrants, from places like India, Bangladesh,

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:03.939
and Haiti.

00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:09.230
They desperately need cash to support their
families and send remittances back home.

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:11.379
These are people at the VERY MARGINS of our
society.

00:04:11.379 --> 00:04:13.299
And how does Uber treat them?

00:04:13.299 --> 00:04:18.560
Uber treats them like a pool of cheap labor,
easy to exploit and then discard.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.220
Uber has always been clear about one thing.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.330
IT DOES NOT CARE about its drivers.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:28.680
At Uber’s greenlight centers, where drivers
register with the company, they were not even

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.449
allowed to enter the bathroom RESERVED for
“employees.”

00:04:31.449 --> 00:04:35.880
In fact, when Uber was talking about how it
would become profitable, it stated clearly

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:40.680
that it wanted to GET RID of its drivers and
transition entirely to self-driving cars.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.740
But Uber’s technology was so bad that after
burning billions of dollars, it had to give

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:45.740
up.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.000
So for now, Uber is stuck with its drivers.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.870
And in order to appear like it’s coming
closer to profitability, it’s been cutting

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:53.930
their wages since 2015.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:58.870
Whenever Uber increases its cut from each
trip or reduces the minimum rate for drivers,

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:01.789
its margins improve by that much immediately.

00:05:01.789 --> 00:05:06.470
As a result, Uber drivers regularly earn LESS
THAN THE MINIMUM WAGE in the places in which

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:07.470
they’re driving.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:11.409
After taking hidden costs like fuel into account,
the average Uber driver earns a little over

00:05:11.409 --> 00:05:16.219
NINE DOLLARS AN HOUR – about 50 percent
LESS than what taxi drivers made before Uber.

00:05:16.219 --> 00:05:21.159
In fact, half of Uber drivers live at or below
the federal poverty line.

00:05:21.159 --> 00:05:24.860
20 percent of them have to use FOOD STAMPS
to survive.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.860
Unable to afford a home, some drivers even
SLEEP IN THEIR CARS.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:34.069
Drivers, with nothing to their name, get trapped
in predatory car rental schemes PROMOTED by

00:05:34.069 --> 00:05:38.270
Uber – they garnish your wages until you’ve
paid it off with interest.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:42.810
One study from Georgetown found that a third
of drivers reported falling into a “debt

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:43.810
trap.”

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:46.020
Some ended up earning less than five dollars
an hour.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:51.319
And because its drivers are technically “independent
contractors,” Uber can avoid pesky requirements

00:05:51.319 --> 00:05:55.639
like minimum wages, health insurance, or paid
sick leave.

00:05:55.639 --> 00:05:58.680
Drivers can’t unionize to bargain for better
conditions.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.930
Uber has ALL the power, and it makes sure
it can stay that way.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:06.380
When California tried to classify drivers
as “employees,” Uber spent hundreds of

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:11.330
millions of dollars on a campaign to overturn
it with a ballot proposition WRITTEN BY UBER’S

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:12.330
LAWYERS.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:17.289
And even though Uber talks about “flexibility,”
that’s not what drivers actually experience.

00:06:17.289 --> 00:06:21.699
Drivers may not have physical bosses to order
them around, but they’re ruled by something

00:06:21.699 --> 00:06:27.539
even worse – an algorithmic overseer that’s
more intrusive than any flesh-and-blood boss

00:06:27.539 --> 00:06:28.539
could ever be.

00:06:28.539 --> 00:06:33.930
Uber’s only actual innovations are in surveilling
and disciplining its workforce.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:40.050
They’re always watching: monitoring driving
behavior, calculating fraud profiles, using

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:44.810
invisible, secret functions to discipline
drivers as well as customers.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.020
Fall below an arbitrary threshold for ratings?

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:48.020
FIRED.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:50.319
Reject too many trips because you don’t
want to lose any money?

00:06:50.319 --> 00:06:51.319
FIRED.

00:06:51.319 --> 00:06:53.360
Did a customer make a false report?

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:54.360
FIRED.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.749
So that’s the “freedom” that Uber offers.

00:06:56.749 --> 00:07:02.789
It frees drivers to make below minimum wage,
to sleep in their cars, to beg for five-star

00:07:02.789 --> 00:07:04.969
ratings and work themselves to the bone.

00:07:04.969 --> 00:07:10.789
But at the end of the day, Uber, like other
“gig” companies, IS an innovator.

00:07:10.789 --> 00:07:13.240
Not in technology, but in exploitation.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:17.479
It’s leading the war against labor in ways
other companies never thought possible.

00:07:17.479 --> 00:07:22.560
It oversees a pool of ATOMIZED, ULTRA-EXPLOITED
laborers, writes its own regulations, and

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:25.740
has done it all without earning a single cent
of profit.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.169
Imagine what sort of world they’ll need
to start making one.

00:07:29.169 --> 00:07:33.809
This is Edward Ongweso, Jr., staff writer
at VICE, for the Gravel Institute.

